WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our next guest is
a legendary television executive

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:05.604
whose memoir "From Saturday
Night to Sunday Night:

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:08.774
My Forty Years of Laughter,
Tears and Touchdowns in TV"

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:10.443
is available now.
Please welcome to the show

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.812
the one, the only
Dick Ebersol, everyone.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.280
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:24.023 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:33.799 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.799 --> 00:00:37.369
Dick, I am so happy to have you
here in 30 Rock,

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:39.238
on the eighth floor,
right down the hall

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:40.973
from "SNL" and studio 8H.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:44.643
You were present at the creation
of "SNL" and then you took over

00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:48.180
the show for five years
after Lorne left for a bit.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.882
And you were there
for Eddie Murphy

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:51.651
and Billy Crystal
and Martin Short.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.853
Is it weird to be
back in the building?

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:55.988
[ Cheers and applause ]
-It is. It is.

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:59.091
I mean, I drove over here
from our apartment in New York

00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:01.760
with my wife and my son,
Charlie.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.065
And I was flashing back to the
first time

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:10.169
that Susan, my wife -- Susan
St. James, hosted the show.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:12.438
And she was holding Charlie
in her arms.

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.207
He had just been born.
[ Audience aws ]

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:18.044
Yeah. You know, that worked
so well for me that, you know,

00:01:18.044 --> 00:01:19.645
Seth's been doing that
for years now.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:22.148
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.951
You talk about and tell a story
in the book

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:26.920
that I'd heard from
the other perspective.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.688
I've heard this story
from Larry David.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.556
It's a famous story,
true to him,

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:33.459
that inspired an episode of
"Seinfeld."

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:36.729
But you can verify the story

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:40.165
about when Larry David quit
"SNL."

00:01:40.165 --> 00:01:44.337
-Well, there's no question that
Larry, over the past 40 years,

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:50.143
is one of the top TV comedy
writers of his time.

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:54.347
But he'd hit a time of, uh,

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.049
not being on top of his game.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.616
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.616 --> 00:02:00.253
And he was a writer/performer
on "Saturday Night Live."

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:03.489
And he'd gone two or three weeks

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:06.159
without getting a sketch
on the show.

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.594
And that's tough when you're
a writer/performer,

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:11.296
if your sketches aren't
getting on the show.

00:02:11.296 --> 00:02:14.033
I was the producer and I was
the one who was judging

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.969
that I didn't think those pieces
would work on the air.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:21.540
And so, at the end of one show,
and I did not witness it --

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.042
I'm sure there are
plenty of people that think

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.011
that's B.S., but I didn't --

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:31.550
and he apparently really let me
have it,

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:34.286
as they were leaving the floor.

00:02:34.286 --> 00:02:36.722
And on Monday, when they had --
Monday or Tuesday,

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:39.358
when they had the writers
meeting in my office,

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:42.195
there was, again,
a lot of tittering.

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:46.866
And I turned to our wonderful
British associate producer,

00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:48.500
and I said,
"Audrey, what's going on?"

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:52.005
She said, "Well, he got
pretty angry with you

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:53.305
at the end of the show
the other night."

00:02:53.305 --> 00:02:54.907
And I said, "What?"

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:57.243
And she said, "Well, he really
let you have it.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:59.378
You know, accused you
of all kinds of things

00:02:59.378 --> 00:03:03.182
for not putting his pieces
on the air."

00:03:03.182 --> 00:03:07.754
So I realized that he needed to
find some way out of this,

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.521
even though he had yet
to come to my office

00:03:09.521 --> 00:03:11.291
and try to have a conversation.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:15.294
But what had happened was
he'd quit the show.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:17.931
And his friends were frantic,
trying to get ahold of him

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:21.133
to tell him, "Wherever you are,
go find Dick and apologize

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:22.468
so you can get back on the air.

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.404
You don't even have
a health plan, for God's sake."

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:27.406
[ Laughter ]
-It is very funny that he quit

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:29.609
and then just came back to work
the next Monday.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:31.176
[ Laughter ]
-Right. Right.

00:03:31.176 --> 00:03:32.512
-And hoped you wouldn't notice.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:34.480
And it seems like
you almost didn't.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.116
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:41.154
-It worked out, and I like to
think it proves that

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:42.521
I'm a forgiving human being.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:44.289
-Yeah, I think so too.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.289 --> 00:03:47.493
The other incredible thing
because you transitioned

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.761
out of comedy
almost directly into sports.

00:03:49.761 --> 00:03:52.498
You have an incredible recall
for details in this book.

00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:55.701
One moment that you talk about,
the best,

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:58.470
the highlight of your career,
in many ways, was

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.040
when you arranged
to have Muhammad Ali

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.575
light the Olympic torch.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.043
Can you tell us about that?
-Yeah.

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:06.279
It's the favorite moment
of my entire career.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:08.514
Comedy, sports, you name it.

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:14.920
My singular moment, for me
at least, was convincing

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.357
the Atlanta organizing
committee, who when I first

00:04:18.357 --> 00:04:20.960
told them I thought that Ali
should light the caldron,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.695
and I was in the position
to do that.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.930
I had made the television rights
deal down there

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.465
for hundreds and hundreds
and hundreds

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.367
and hundreds of millions
of dollars.

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:31.370
So I figured, you know,
they'd think this was fine.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.006
And they said to me,
"No, no, he's a draft dodger."

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.942
And I just about blew up
at them.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:37.242
I said,
"What are you talking about?

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:38.811
He was not a draft dodger.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:42.381
He was a man of great moral
courage who stood on that.

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:44.083
Stood on that."

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:49.989
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:54.460
He gave up so much money and he
went through a lot of hell

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.961
in dealing with the government.

00:04:55.961 --> 00:05:00.065
But the irony is he was judged

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:02.635
by the United States
Supreme Court

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:06.105
to be innocent 8-0.

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:08.473
And it was
a very conservative court.

00:05:08.473 --> 00:05:14.147
So he was totally allowed then
to resume his career,

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.616
no thanks to the Atlanta
organizing committee.

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:20.586
But a couple of years ago,
I heard one of those members

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:25.991
of the Atlanta organizing
committee say that,

00:05:25.991 --> 00:05:29.027
"You know, it's too bad
what he had to go through."

00:05:29.027 --> 00:05:31.196
And I said, "Too bad?

00:05:31.196 --> 00:05:32.732
You're the ones who put him
through it."

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.767
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:38.370
Anyway, a couple days after -- a
week after the games were over,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:43.275
I got a phone call
from his now-widow, Lonnie Ali.

00:05:43.275 --> 00:05:46.112
And she said, "I'm standing
outside of a building"

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:49.315
at a famous golf course in
Atlanta where they were playing

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:51.016
some championship event.

00:05:51.016 --> 00:05:54.186
And you heard
all this chanting and yelling.

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:56.254
And she said, "Dick, I just want
you to hear this

00:05:56.254 --> 00:06:00.426
because this is what kept him
out of the public eye.

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:03.729
He was too embarrassed to put
himself out there, thinking that

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:07.332
people would boo him or not like
him or something like that.

00:06:07.332 --> 00:06:12.171
And you and your team gave him
back his life."

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:17.042
And I started -- The tears
started to run down my cheek.

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:23.449
And I thought of how much
he'd missed out on, and now,

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.150
when I think about it
afterwards,

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:26.585
he didn't feel that way.

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:29.021
-Well, that's a pretty great
best moment in television.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:32.124
I think very few measure
up to that.

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:36.929
You also talk/write quite
beautifully about tragedy.

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:38.597
You lost your son Teddy

00:06:38.597 --> 00:06:40.899
when he was 14 years old
in a plane crash.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:46.138
Did you find -- and you write,
again, very sweetly about

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:48.707
what a full life you had,
how happy you were

00:06:48.707 --> 00:06:51.043
to have 14 years with him.

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:53.446
Was this more
for your own catharsis?

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:54.980
Or were you trying to give this
as a gift

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.883
to other parents
when you wrote about that?

00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.085
-I think the latter.

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:01.821
I find as a parent, my wife

00:07:01.821 --> 00:07:03.589
obviously was part of this.

00:07:03.589 --> 00:07:06.025
She bore him.

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:12.464
But we felt so fortunate to have
had him for so long,

00:07:12.464 --> 00:07:15.267
and we tell people now,
when we're asked to speak

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:19.105
or whatever, we think
it's really important within

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:23.976
your own family, grandchildren,
senior grandchildren,

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:27.913
you name it, for them
to really know that kid.

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:30.215
And in our family, it's amazing

00:07:30.215 --> 00:07:33.218
where there's I think 14 or 15
grandchildren,

00:07:33.218 --> 00:07:37.890
ranging down to ages like 1, 2,
3, up to age 14,

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.525
and they talk about him.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.593
They talk about him
as if they knew him

00:07:41.593 --> 00:07:43.628
because they've heard
all the stories.

00:07:43.628 --> 00:07:46.231
And that is cathartic.

00:07:46.231 --> 00:07:51.636
I mean, it really means that
you don't have to give up

00:07:51.636 --> 00:07:56.041
on somebody who is such a part
of your life just because

00:07:56.041 --> 00:07:57.843
they physically aren't there
anymore.

00:07:57.843 --> 00:08:00.813
There are memories to be
cherished forever

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:02.314
and to be discussed forever.

00:08:02.314 --> 00:08:06.018
And I can't emphasize enough to
families who are going through

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:09.622
this today or have already,
talk about it.

00:08:09.622 --> 00:08:13.392
It's so important to keep
those spirits alive.

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:15.460
-Well, it's a wonderful message.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:16.862
It's a wonderful book.

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:19.231
And just thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:19.231 --> 00:08:21.734
It feels very fitting to have
you back at 30 Rock.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.068
Thank you so much.
Dick Ebersol, everybody.

00:08:23.068 --> 00:08:25.671
-Thank you, Seth, very much.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:27.373
-"From Saturday Night
to Sunday Night"

00:08:27.373 --> 00:08:29.008
is available now
wherever books are sold.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:31.009
But please support your local
and independent bookstores.

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:32.044
We'll be right back.

